Overview
In this assignment, you will brainstorm a premise for the original short narrative story you’ve been developing in the course. You will learn how writers determine their story’s premise. This assignment will help prepare you to complete the development of the original short narrative story you have been working on in this course.

Instructions
1. Review the instructional content below.

What Is a Premise?

Whether intentionally or unintentionally, every story you create or tell has a purpose. That purpose is what we call a premise. A premise is what your story proves to the audience, and it’s completely linked to what you — as an artist and human being — believe to be true about the world, and what you want to tell the world.

For this assignment, you’ll read/view the following, then enter a short response below to demonstrate your understanding of the concepts presented.     

Let’s start by taking a look at some examples of premises from well-known works of fiction:

William Shakespeare’s Macbeth: “Ruthless ambition leads to its own destruction.”
William Shakespeare’s Romeo and Juliet: “Great love defeats even death.”
The Three Little Pigs: “Foolishness leads to death, and wisdom leads to happiness.”
The Tortoise and the Hare: “Slow and steady wins the race.”
Witness (1985 film): “Love fails when the two lovers are from different worlds.”
How Is a Premise Different from a Theme?

The premise refers to the idea behind the story and can be used as a storytelling tool. For instance, an author could ask the following questions to determine the premise of the story he or she wants to tell: “What is the main concept behind my story?” “What am I trying to prove?” “What message do I want to tell the world?”

The theme is the audience’s interpretation of what the story really means (to them). It is the underlying emotion or takeaway message behind the heart of the story and is often expressed in just one or two words. The premise can be stated as a full sentence and describes the plot.

Let’s take another look at our example from earlier — William Shakespeare’s Romeo and Juliet:

Premise		Theme	
Great love defeats even death.

Love

The author wants to send this message/prove this point to the world/audience.

This is the underlying emotion or takeaway message behind the heart of the story.

Answer at least one of the following questions based on your story. Your answers should help you brainstorm ideas to form your premise:

What is the main concept behind my story?
What message do I want to tell the world?
What statement am I trying to prove?
4. Answer all of the following based on your story. Your answers should help you brainstorm ideas to form your premise:

What is a trait of my main character that could lead to a premise?
What is the main conflict of my story?
What is the conclusion of my story?
Write at least one possible premise for your story.

